The generic source transport and the original four-family source/configuration
corpus are now qualified offline. This is a prerequisite result: the new
original-repository caller, scorer/publication path, lifecycle reuse and
procedure integration have not run as a combined cohort. No provider or scorer
calls were made by these controls. The completed Requests cohorts remain
unchanged.

`original-corpus-v2/corpus.json` retains the four original development families,
six events per trajectory, all four arms, both local cache profiles and seven
warm one-factor controls. The proposed full matrix remains 32 primary plus 28
one-factor trajectories, or 360 top-level event observations. The first tranche
remains the first two families under cold A–D. Four provider calls per trajectory
remain a provisional ceiling encompassing every purpose and priming; this
corpus does not enable dispatch or establish exact call identities.

| Event | Public input and required behavior |
| --- | --- |
| 1 | Acquire the explicit public objective and compile obligations; independent plan-coverage qualification remains open. |
| 2 | Attempt the original repair against the complete independent acceptance criteria. |
| 3 | Revisit unchanged accepted source, or unchanged public input after a failed repair. Every arm gets ordinary current-source/no-op checks. |
| 4 | Append the frozen public comment once to the admitted repair module and revalidate. |
| 5 | Apply the exact exposed API configuration change and revalidate. |
| 6 | Validate, interrupt after durable seal evidence before parent CAS, recover in a fresh process, attempt a separately identified stale-parent branch, then consume the recovered parent in a fresh continuation. Unreached boundaries remain explicit dependent failures. |

The API changes are namespace processing true→false for XML, recursive
whitespace true→false for Pyparsing, an explicit metadata response-type claim
for OAuth, and `show_choices` false→true for Click. Each has canonical JSON and
a separate hash. The original visible/private checks and all four/nine exposed
public objective examples remain mandatory; passing the changed configuration
alone cannot satisfy the repair. Objective record and objective text hashes are
distinct. XML, OAuth and Click objectives are newly authored development
contracts; Pyparsing binds the retrieved public issue. Historical issue wording
is not claimed for the other families.

Failure carry is executable in `original_sequence_v1.py`: use the immediately
preceding event's accepted source when that event accepted, otherwise its exact
public input. That input already carries earlier accepted changes and public
transformations. An old accepted publication pointer cannot roll back a later
comment after validation fails. Rejected candidates never become input, and the
original failed-repair receipt stays in downstream dependency records. The
recovery schedule cannot manufacture a passing candidate to reach its fault
boundary. Actual native interruption, stale CAS and continuation still need
the integrated caller qualification.

`repository_runtime_v2.py` reconstructs every candidate from immutable base
bytes plus its complete cumulative content patch. The text proposal interface
has no mode channel. The original adapter preserves authoritative base modes;
Requests/Zarr use exact Git application under umask 0022. Thus reverting all
content restores the complete pristine inventory, including raw modes. All
three retained Requests-v2 mode-only failures reproduce this correction in new
copies; their original failures and costs remain intact, and their
counterfactual grades remain unknown. Inventory wire formats stay explicit:
original path-keyed S_IMODE/size entries, Requests masked-mode list entries and
Zarr raw `stat.st_mode` entries are different hashes.

The Git carry helper authenticates both old and new tracked public trees
against their real upstream commits before three-way merging accepted changes.
Only public export blobs populate the local graph. Image-generated Zarr version
modules are separately bound overlays, excluded from claims about tracked Git
content and replaced with the exact new-base overlay. Conflicts retain complete
source markers. A failed predecessor permits the declared public update while
preserving its failure. A mismatched new export is rejected even if its local
source-policy hash is recomputed to match.

`repository_retrieval_v2.py` applies the same mandatory-first policy to explicit
per-task source paths. Complete mandatory modules must fit the file, raw-byte
and actual serialized wire bounds; optional lexical modules fill only remaining
space. Expansions retain mandatory modules and reject oversize complete inputs.
All paths are normalized and contained, symlink components are rejected for
source reads, and exact bytes/hashes are rechecked after ranking. Sources are
quiescent trusted filesystem inputs; concurrent hostile filesystem behavior is
not qualified.

| Retained artifact | Observed result |
| --- | --- |
| `generic-transport-controls-v1/summary.json` | 138 passing offline checks; retained before independent review identified missing new-base Git authentication. |
| `generic-transport-controls-v2/summary.json` | 144 passing checks in 18.652436 seconds over eight public bases, with both upstream trees authenticated and negative mismatched-export controls. Includes actual Requests/Zarr Git carry, conflicts and failed-parent advance. |
| `original-source-branch-controls-v1/summary.json` | 323 passing checks over 72 source/configuration transitions on four real repositories. Acceptance flags are declared transport fixtures, not observed repairs or graders. |
| `generic_transport_admission_v1.json` | 283 passing read-only joins of current/frozen service bytes, complete source inventories, configurations, patch identities and fixture records. Qualified for source transport only; live dispatch remains disabled. |

`original-corpus-v1` is retained. Version 2 clarifies that a failed public
comment event must not roll its source input back through an older accepted
pointer; objective and configuration files did not change. The first transport
qualification and frozen source copies are also retained after adding the
independent new-base check. No result was overwritten.

The original scorer endpoint remains independently gated. In particular, the
curator reports an OAuth incompatibility between the saved historical oracle
and the newly exposed combined-server contract. These transport controls do not
resolve it or authorize changing criteria. The context agent is separately
implementing a native validation-action procedure and lifecycle reuse adapter;
the qualified arithmetic TEST procedure is not represented as repository repair
integration. Any native suggestion must name the exact current scorer request,
and any CALL reuse must preserve current setup, teardown and whole-item outcome.
